{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The wizard that showed a spinner and an error at the same time</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A multi-step signup wizard tracked its submit step with a handful of booleans: <code>isSubmitting</code>, <code>hasError</code>, <code>isComplete</code>. A slow network plus a quick retry click produced a screen showing a loading spinner and a red error banner stacked on top of each other, because a retry set <code>isSubmitting</code> back to <code>true</code> without anyone remembering to also reset <code>hasError</code> to <code>false</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Three independent booleans describe eight possible combinations. The UI's render logic only ever intentionally handles maybe four of those — loading, error, success, idle — but nothing in the type system or the code stops the other four from happening. They're not commented as invalid, they're just never supposed to occur, which is a much weaker guarantee.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is boolean explosion: <em>n</em> independent flags describe 2<sup>n</sup> possible combinations, and almost every real UI only has a handful of states that are actually meaningful. The other combinations aren't edge cases to handle, they're states that shouldn't exist at all — but scattered booleans have no way to say that.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Name the real states, then only allow moving between them</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Instead of several independent true/false flags, use one variable that holds exactly one named state at a time: <code>idle</code>, <code>submitting</code>, <code>success</code>, or <code>error</code>. Since it's one variable, it's structurally impossible for the UI to be in two of those at once — there's no such thing as a "submitting and error" value if <code>state</code> can only ever equal one string.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A finite state machine adds one more piece: an explicit list of which transitions are even allowed from each state — <code>idle</code> can go to <code>submitting</code>, <code>submitting</code> can go to <code>success</code> or <code>error</code>, <code>error</code> can go back to <code>submitting</code> on retry. An event that isn't a valid transition from the current state simply doesn't fire, instead of quietly corrupting the state.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is the difference between a state <em>value</em> (a single variable holding one of a few named states — better than scattered booleans, but nothing stops any transition from anywhere) and a state <em>machine</em> (the transition table itself is data, so "can you even get from A to B" is answerable by inspection, and libraries like XState can visualize and even statically verify it, catching an unreachable or dead-end state before it ships).
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The checkout flow that could "complete" twice</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A checkout wizard used <code>isProcessingPayment</code>, <code>paymentFailed</code>, and <code>orderComplete</code> booleans across three steps. A user whose payment failed, then retried and succeeded, ended up on a screen where the "order complete" confirmation and a leftover "payment failed, please try again" banner from the previous attempt were both still visible, because the retry's success handler set <code>orderComplete</code> without anyone remembering that <code>paymentFailed</code> was still <code>true</code> from before.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The team rebuilt the flow's step state as a single machine: <code>idle → processing → (failed | complete)</code>, with <code>failed → processing</code> as the only way back in. The render logic switched from "check three booleans and hope" to a single <code>switch</code> on one state value, and the leftover-banner bug became impossible to reproduce because there was no longer a code path where two conflicting states could both be true.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      They also found, while drawing out the transition table, a state their old code silently allowed that made no product sense at all: retrying payment from the <code>complete</code> state, left over from a stray button that hadn't been disabled after success. The FSM model forced them to explicitly decide "no, <code>complete</code> has no outgoing transitions" — a decision the boolean version had never actually been forced to make.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

