{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>The transition table is the whole design surface</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Writing the transition table down (even as a plain object, no library required) forces you to make every decision explicit up front: what can happen from each state, and just as importantly, what deliberately can't. That second half — the states you can't reach from a given point — is usually where the real bugs were hiding in the boolean version.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A hand-rolled <code>switch</code> on a single state string gets you most of the benefit for a simple wizard. Reach for a library like XState once you need parallel states (two independent sub-machines running at once), hierarchical states (a "submitting" state that's itself broken into sub-steps), or you want the transition table to be visually diagrammable for a design/product conversation.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Don't let the state value leak implementation booleans back in through the side door — a common half-measure is keeping <code>state: 'submitting' | 'error' | ...</code> alongside a still-separate <code>isDirty</code> or <code>retryCount</code> flag that can drift out of sync with it. Model anything that genuinely depends on which state you're in as part of that state (or as machine "context" data scoped to the current state), not as a sibling variable with its own independent lifecycle.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

